Z||000023||theogre||12-21-1999||07:15 PM||||Delorean did work in one of GM's design centers as I recall. I don't think he was involved in Fiero.<P>Acording to my information only 2 DMC's came in other than raw stainless. These were both gold plated. All others are aftermarket paint.<P>Acording to some recent news, Delorean is getting up another car company.<P>DMC was on its way to bankruptcy court before John's arrest. Drugs do that. It was a shame no one had the forsight to buy it as a whole and keep them going.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Wolfhound||12-21-1999||07:52 PM||||DeLorean left Pontiac in the mid-70's to start his own company. His bankruptcy was not caused by drugs, but rather made him easy to be set up by the Feds who offered him a apparent out. He was acquited by a jury, that agreed that it was intrapment, but in the process of defending himself DMC was finally lost. One of the two Gold Deloreans is inside a glass case in the lobby of a bank in Texas ,zero miles.<BR>  Possibly more politics were involved in this than meets the eye.<BR>(Just because your paranoid doesn't mean someones not after you!) ||||reg||1
Z||000025||bHooper||12-21-1999||08:13 PM||||well, well, well...picking on poor johnny boy, are we?<P>let me step up to the mans defence, somewhat.  When DMC first started production in 1981, they had an agreement with the english government.   This "agreement" basically stated that each Delorean was "presold", meaning that england would put up the full sales price for each car that came off of the assembly line... at least until the company could turn a production and a proffit to sustain itself.    After setting up a dealer network in the USA (strangely enough at quite a few GM dealerships), and shipping the cars, mother england failed to make the promissed payments.  Much of this was set up and done in order to provide industry to Ireland... the only reason Delorean went there.  After a year and a half of production, and running out of capital, J.D. tryed to attract other investors, allways to no avail.  Finally he was introduced to several individuals, being told that they could help finance his company.  For some reason he did have his suspicions about the people, but upon the advice of his lawyer, he was told to go to the meeting and then refuse their service.  Unfortunately, that never happened in time and the rest is history.<P>Personal opinion about th whole thing... J.D. was inocent, but set up by a colaboration between the GM (our old pal trusty Roger Smith), the English government (cant have an american build a car in Ireland and outsell British Leyland (it was a bad year for the govt owned auto industry, afterall)), and the US govt being drug into it by both of the above...  <P>whew, my fingers are tired   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I like J.D.!  The man is an automotive genious!  And best of all...Roger Smith hated him too... ain't nothing wrong with that...Fiero!!!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 12-21-1999).]||||reg||12
Z||000026||COUNTIBLIS||12-21-1999||08:20 PM||||Huh?  This is all too interesting I didnt know that is went that far in depth.  I myself always liked the car, I thought it always looked like a Fiero.  As far as J.D. making another car company I wish him luck.  Now as Paul Harvey would say on with the news.||||reg||1
Z||000027||COUNTIBLIS||12-21-1999||08:22 PM||||BTW.....Who is Roger Smith?  Didnt he kill the fiero?||||reg||1
