Z||000037||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||11:55 PM||||It would be next to impossible to simply polish a Delorean to make it shine like glass.  The Delorean is brushed SS, which is only about 2 steps up from raw.  You can, however, do something called "electro-polish".  That is what gives some SS the glassy shine.  (Kinda like my eyes about 15 min after medicine time)  As far as the plastic bumper go.  If you're gonna spend $15000 (yes. I said 15 large.  It costs about $15 to e-polish a 1" dia pipe 2' long) to electro-polish the car, you might as well spend another couple grand to have SS bumpers made.  Just a thought for those of you who have more money than sense.||||reg||1
Z||000038||DJRice||12-23-1999||12:05 AM||||It would be cool to then take the car to the flea market and get it gold electroplated or chrome electroplated.||||reg||1
Z||000039||XenoGen||12-26-1999||12:53 PM||||Another Long Post By Me:<BR> <BR> Bricklin? Did I hear someone mention a Bricklin?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Neat, I did not know that John Delorean based his car off of the Bricklin...<P> Okay - As I stated in my first message that you may have missed, I'm new and I like unique cars. I don't have a lot of car knowledge, but I try to learn what I can. <P>MY Point:<P> I've known that Bricklins exist for a little over a year now. While looking through car magazines, I came accross a picture of a strange car for sale. It said it was a Bricklin. I immediatly thought it looked really neat. My father told me that those cars had gullwing doors, like the Delorean. I thought that was sooo neat. Anyways, afterawhile the Bricklin became one of my favorite cars. If I was rich, I think I'd favor that car over a Ferrari or Lamborghinni, because of its uniqueness and its styling. I find the Bricklin is styled just as nice as the 90's sports cars. And to think, it's a 70's car... I personally think the makers were years ahead of their time with the Bricklin's shape and sense of style. I find it retains that exotic look, even after 25 years. <P> About two months ago, my friend and I were driving through a rather rural part of the city, when I saw an orange car parked in someone's lawn. I yelled out "oh my god, it's a Bricklin!" My friend was like "Oh my god, what is THAT???" And I said "It's a Bricklin!" He askes: "It's a what???"  It was the first time I saw a Bricklin in person, so I was rather excited.<P>I thought to myself "Imagine... A Bricklin in MY city! What are the chances?"  (Acxcording to Bricklin International, there were 2854 cars built with an estimated 1500 still in existance today)  The Bricklin was parked on some guy's lawn, so we pulled into his driveway and ran to the car. I looked it over a couple of times before knocking on the guys door to ask him about his car. He wasn't very nice to us, but he did tell us we could look at it. <P> Anyways, I was pretty happy with that. My friend loved the car as well. He had never even heard of the car before. <P> When we got to school, we searched the internet for facts about the Bricklin. We both thought it was one of the nestest cars we'd ever seen. So when we read about who made the car and designed it, and where it was created and manufactured, we both freaked. The car was born about 200 kms away from where we lived, and manufactured about an hour's drive away from our house. That was such a big surprise -  I had NO idea that Canada ever designed a car, let alone created the Bricklin, my favorite car at the time. Still my favorite, in a way. And we're both very proud of that car now, cause it's part of our province's history.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> It was an interesting fact to find. I'm still amazed, really.<P>Anyways, I just thought I'd share this little story about the Bricklin, since it was mentioned. <P> Also, if anyone has any more info or sites on the Bricklin, I'd like to hear about them. I wonder if the designers still live in the area? Cause I'd sure love to ask them some questions! And I'm still not entirely sure why the Bricklins had to be cancelled. If anyone else has any interest in the Bricklin and its history, I might do some digging around and see what I can find around here and share the info. Anywyas, let me know if anyone cares about the Bricklin. I love the Fiero a lot too, but I can't help but still have strong feelings for the Bricklin.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000040||Monkeyman||12-26-1999||07:48 PM||||XenoGen--The designer of the Bricklin (Malcolm Bricklin) is dead.  Sorry.  One of the reasons (and I'm sure there are many) the Bricklins bit the dust is/was because of the doors.  The doors are, indeed, gullwings, but they are/were air operated.  You push a button and the door opens, you push the button again and it closes.  Cool until you develop and air leak.  Then you can't get into your car.  Worse yet if you're already in the car and can't get out.||||reg||1
